data.content:
These are two distinct C# 15 breaking changes:

---

### 1. `&&` with `dynamic` and interface operators

The `&&` operator desugars to `operator true(left) ? left : left & right`. When `right` is `dynamic`, the `&` is resolved at **runtime** by the dynamic binder. However, the runtime binder **cannot resolve user-defined operators declared on interfaces** — it only understands operators on classes/structs.

In C# 15, the compiler now correctly rejects this combination at compile time rather than letting it fail at runtime.

**Fixes** (pick one):
```csharp
// Option A: avoid dynamic, use explicit type
void Apply(IFilter left, IFilter right) { ... }

// Option B: separate the short-circuit logic manually
void Apply(IFilter left, dynamic right)
{
    if (IFilter.op_True(left))
    {
        var combined = left;
    }
    else
    {
        IFilter r = right;        // runtime cast
        var combined = left & r;   // resolved statically
    }
}
```

---

### 2. `ref readonly` lambda — CS0518

When you write:
```csharp
var fn = ref readonly int () => ref value;
```

the compiler must synthesize an anonymous delegate type (no built-in `Func<>` supports `ref readonly` returns). In .NET 11, the compiler requires the predefined attribute type `System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiresLocationAttribute` to mark the `ref readonly` return on the synthesized delegate — and if your project's reference assemblies don't include it (e.g., targeting an older TFM, or a stale SDK restore), you get **CS0518**.

**Fixes:**
```csharp
// Option A: ensure you're targeting net11.0
<TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>

// Option B: declare an explicit delegate type instead of var
delegate ref readonly int RefReadonlyFunc();
RefReadonlyFunc fn = ref readonly int () => ref value;
```

---

**TL;DR:** (1) The dynamic binder doesn't understand interface-defined operators — avoid mixing `dynamic` with `&&` on interface types. (2) The `ref readonly` lambda needs `RequiresLocationAttribute` from the runtime — make sure your TFM and SDK are both .NET 11.
